# M4.25.1 duplicate systemd scalar guard

Date: 2026-08-28
Scope: PR #34 stacked integration review only
Status: corrected locally; no server action performed

## Finding

The M4.25 unit itself contains the intended single values `Restart=no` and
`RuntimeMaxSec=180`. The pre-install release check and its regression test,
however, counted only those exact safe lines and rejected only selected unsafe
restart values. A second scalar assignment such as `Restart=on-abnormal` or
`RuntimeMaxSec=infinity` could therefore coexist with the required line and
pass the pre-install text guard. systemd applies the effective scalar value,
so a later assignment could override the safe value.

The post-install effective-property check would still fail, but only after the
unit had been replaced and daemon-reloaded. That violates the intended
fail-closed pre-mutation boundary and would require the already documented
manual rollback path.

## Minimal correction

The release controller now requires exactly one `Restart=` assignment and
exactly one `RuntimeMaxSec=` assignment, with the respective exact values
`no` and `180`. It also rejects `RestartSec=`, `OnFailure=`, `[Install]` and
`WantedBy=` before installation. The unit test and CI static guard enforce the
same exact scalar cardinality.

The versioned systemd unit, M4.24 historical contract, M4.25 evidence contract,
unit digest, backup evidence and restore evidence are unchanged. No service,
server, provider, token, payment, publishing or first-start action is involved.

## Regression proof

The M4.25 test must fail if any second `Restart=` or `RuntimeMaxSec=` assignment
is added, including values not listed by the previous denylist. The full
Control suite, shell syntax, JSON/schema checks, cumulative merge simulation
and remote CI must pass again before PR #34 can receive a merge recommendation.

## Rollback

Before merge, revert the dedicated M4.25.1 guard commit. No operational
rollback is needed because this correction changes only unmerged Control
source, tests, CI and this diagnosis. First Start remains `NO-GO`.
